 or attacked by  police now this is as you can imagine  dangerous because these rules can be  misused police can incite a couple of  people to break the law to then justify  clearing an entire  protests that's a way in which police  can misuse these rules but understand  that when part of a protest has gone bad  legally I'm not saying morally legally  police can clear the entire area now in  terms of where you can protest I  mentioned highways could be off-limits  that would not be considered a  limitation of your First Amendment  rights if police say you can't walk on  the highway and do your protests there  there are states that require permits if  you're going to be in the street or  sometimes on the sidewalk if you're not  going to be moving and you're going to  be blocking traffic now a pedestrian  traffic generally you do not need  permits to protest while walking along  sidewalks or even in streets sometimes  if you are not obstructing traffic  depends on the state it's very hard to  protest on a road without obstructing  traffic so the road protests almost  always do need a permit generally  speaking public spaces can be protest  locations that include sidewalks and  parks and the like doesn't mean you can  do it without a permit in some states  you must know the laws of your state  anytime that you block the passage of  people or cars and this includes on  sidewalks you can be arrested if you  refuse to move understand that and again  this can be misused by police police  will sometimes say you're blocking  pedestrian traffic and then say and  because of that it's time to wrap it up  and then you stay and you get arrested  even though traffic may not actually  have been blocked police are supposed to  provide a reasonable opportunity to  comply and that includes sufficient time  and a clear unobstructed exit path we  had reports from yesterday that  protesters were sort of corralled onto  one of the bridges in between Manhattan  and Brooklyn I believe the Manhattan  Bridge and that would be in violation of  if you say it's time to go you have to  provide an unobstructed exit path and  time to exit now this means that they  can't say the party's over and within 15  seconds start arresting people but it is  sometimes misused by police to claim we  issued a dispersal order and they didn't  move  um quick note about government buildings  government buildings can be exceptions  they there's sort of public buildings in  a sense that doesn't mean you can  necessarily protest inside and that  varies by state very important if you  want to show up and record with your  phone or with a camera or whatever you  can record what is going on in public  and you can record police no matter what  they tell you again the trick to watch  out for is that if by recording you are  impeding police where you've decided to  stand if you're getting in the way of  what police are doing they can argue you  are interfering with police you're  obstructing law enforcement as you can  imagine that can also be misused and you  can find examples of police saying that  somebody who's really not obstructing is  obstructing by virtue of where they're  standing to record the advice we  generally see is stand on the sidewalk  to record if the altercation is  happening on the sidewalk make sure that  you're not in the way of police now in  terms of recording you are also  protesting in a public space meaning you  can be recorded your picture can be  taken your face could be published in  media without your consent understand  that when you go to a protest you're not  starting an altercation because  someone's filming you but they do film  you and then that is used somewhere that  is legal because you are out in public  at a protest police do have pretty broad  rights to maintain law and order and  uphold local laws and this can sometimes  be Mis applied to filming be aware  police can't tell you that you have to  turn over your phone they can't force  you to show them stuff on your phone  they need a warrant signed by a judge in  order to do that now if police approach  you because you are recording certainly  remind the police that recording in a  public space is number one of course  legal and number two does not count as  reasonable suspicion to believe you have  committed a crime in other words if  police say there they're approaching you  because you're acting suspiciously and  all you're doing is filming filming does  not count  as reasonable suspicion if you do break  the law and get a  austan the normal search and seizure  rules would apply if police tell you  you've got a deleted video from your  phone that is not a lawful order from  police and you don't have to do it if  police want to search your backpack or  your wallet or your fanny pack before or  after an arrest you're under no  obligation to allow it and you can even  say I do not consent to a search of my  property  police typically are allowed to Pat you  down  in some states you do have to give  police your name but you are not  required to give any other information  remember counter-protesters that might  not agree with you also have free speech  rights and police are supposed to treat  both protesters and counter protesters  equally according to the ACLU police are  permitted if they identify antagonistic  groups police are permitted to keep  those groups separated but they should  allow them to be within sight and sound  of each other otherwise you're not  really able to protest you're not really  able to counter protest if you can only  do it out of earshot and visible range  of the people you are protesting so then  a few notes on police interactions if  police approached you and you and want  to talk to you you don't have to talk to  them but don't resist of course it's  better not to run away but simply ask am  I being detained or am I free to go if  they say you were not free to go you are  able to inquire what is the crime I'm  suspected o